WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.670
Partitioning lets different users, tenants, clients, routes, or other groups have separate counters.

2
00:00:06.670 --> 00:00:12.806
Without partitioning, one busy client can consume a shared limit and affect unrelated users.

3
00:00:12.806 --> 00:00:21.076
With partitioning, a policy can be fairer because it tracks request flow against a meaningful identity or workload category.

4
00:00:21.076 --> 00:00:30.347
Possible partition keys include authenticated user, tenant ID, API key, client application, route group, host, or a network-derived signal.

5
00:00:30.347 --> 00:00:31.881
Each key has tradeoffs.

6
00:00:31.881 --> 00:00:35.149
A user key may work well for signed-in workflows.

7
00:00:35.149 --> 00:00:40.018
A tenant key may protect shared backend capacity in multi-tenant systems.

8
00:00:40.018 --> 00:00:44.419
A route key may separate expensive endpoints from cheap endpoints.

9
00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:48.488
An API key may represent a client application or integration.

10
00:00:48.488 --> 00:00:53.690
Partition keys should be stable, meaningful, privacy-aware, and hard to spoof.

11
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:58.359
If the key changes frequently, the limit may not represent real usage.

12
00:00:58.359 --> 00:01:03.228
If the key is too broad, unrelated callers may interfere with each other.

13
00:01:03.228 --> 00:01:09.230
If the key contains sensitive data, logs and metrics may expose information unnecessarily.

14
00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:14.232
If the key is easy to forge, the policy may not protect the intended group.

15
00:01:14.232 --> 00:01:17.634
A single global counter can punish unrelated users.

16
00:01:17.634 --> 00:01:25.904
For example, one tenant's high-volume reporting activity should not necessarily consume all capacity for every other tenant.

17
00:01:25.904 --> 00:01:36.109
Fairness is especially important when backend resources are shared, such as databases, queues, search indexes, storage accounts, or third-party services.

18
00:01:36.109 --> 00:01:41.778
Unauthenticated requests require special care because identity may be weak or absent.

19
00:01:41.778 --> 00:01:54.784
Public endpoints may rely on route, host, client application, network-derived signals, or other context, but those keys should be treated as lower confidence than a strong authenticated identity.

20
00:01:54.784 --> 00:02:01.453
The policy should reflect that uncertainty and avoid storing unnecessary personal or sensitive data.

21
00:02:01.453 --> 00:02:06.522
The practical design question is, who or what should be protected from whom.

22
00:02:06.522 --> 00:02:09.457
Good partitioning makes that answer visible.

23
00:02:09.457 --> 00:02:18.927
It turns rate limiting from one global throttle into a fairness and resource-protection policy that matches the application's real boundaries.
